{:edb/db-before-t 1535 :edb/db-after-t 1536 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534848 50 #inst "2026-10-08T08:59:19.935Z" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119624 1100 "Find a vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=y^5-x^2$." 13194139534848 false] [17592186119624 1100 "Find a vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=y^5-x^2.$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119624 1145 "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + z \\, \\mathbf{k} = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\big(y^5-x^2\\big) \\, \\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v$, we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = u \\, \\mathbf{i} + v \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\big(v^5-u^2\\big) \\, \\mathbf{k}\n$$" 13194139534848 false] [17592186119624 1145 "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + z \\, \\mathbf{k} = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\big(y^5-x^2\\big) \\, \\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = u \\, \\mathbf{i} + v \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\big(v^5-u^2\\big) \\, \\mathbf{k}\n$$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119625 1100 "$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) =\\big\\langle u, \\: v, \\:  \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n$x^2z-2y+z=6.$\n What is the third component of $\\mathbf{r}?$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119625 1100 "$\\mathbf{r}(u,v) =\\big\\langle u, \\: v, \\: \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n$x^2z-2y+z=6$.\nWhat is the third component of $\\mathbf{r}$?" 13194139534848 false] [17592186119625 1145 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}z - 2y + z &= 6 \\\\\n(x^{2} + 1)z &= 6 + 2y \\\\\nz &= (2(y + 3))/(x^{2} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\left\\langle x, \\: y, \\: \\dfrac{2(y+3)}{x^2+1} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v$, we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) =\\left\\langle u, \\: v, \\: {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2(v+3)}{u^2+1}} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{r}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}\\dfrac{2(v+3)}{u^2+1}}\n$$" 13194139534848 false] [17592186119625 1145 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}z-2y+z & =6 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)z & =6+2y \\\\ z & =\\frac{2(y+3)}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\left\\langle x, \\: y, \\: \\dfrac{2(y+3)}{x^2+1} \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) =\\left\\langle u, \\: v, \\:  {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2(v+3)}{u^2+1}} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{r}$ is ${\\color{black}\\dfrac{2(v+3)}{u^2+1}}.$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119626 1100 "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =\\big\\langle r\\cos{\\theta}, \\: r\\sin{\\theta}, \\: \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n${r^2}-4z^2=0$\nfor $z \\geq 0.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119626 1100 "$\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =\\big\\langle r\\cos{\\theta}, \\: r\\sin{\\theta}, \\: \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as\n${r^2}-4z^2=0$\nfor $z \\geq 0$. What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" 13194139534848 false] [17592186119626 1145 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form\n\n$$\nz=f(r,\\theta)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} - 4z^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n4z^{2} &= r^{2} \\\\\nz^{2} &= \\frac{r^{2}}{4} \\\\\nz &= \\frac{r}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that\n\n$$\nz\\geq 0\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad z = z\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle r\\cos\\theta, \\: r\\sin\\theta, \\: z \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nz= \\dfrac r2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =\\left\\langle r\\cos{\\theta}, \\: r\\sin{\\theta}, \\: {\\color{black} \\dfrac r2} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\color{black} \\dfrac r2}\n$$" 13194139534848 false] [17592186119626 1145 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(r,\\theta)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2}-4{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =\\frac{{r}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z & =\\frac{r}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z\\geq 0.$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle r\\cos\\theta, \\: r\\sin\\theta, \\: z \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z= \\dfrac r2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =\\left\\langle r\\cos{\\theta}, \\: r\\sin{\\theta}, \\: {\\color{black}  \\dfrac r2} \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}  \\dfrac r2}.$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119627 1100 "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: \\theta \\sin\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi, \\: \\theta \\sin\\phi \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi}.$\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119627 1100 "$\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: \\theta \\sin\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi, \\: \\theta \\sin\\phi \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as\n$\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi}$.\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" 13194139534848 false] [17592186119627 1145 "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}\\theta \\cos\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi}.$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119627 1145 "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\n\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(θ, ϕ) &= ⟨θ\\sin ϕ \\cdot \\cos θ\\sin ϕ, θ\\sin ϕ \\cdot \\sin θ\\sin ϕ, θ\\sin ϕ \\cdot \\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨θ\\cos θ\\sin^{2} ϕ, θ\\sin θ\\sin^{2} ϕ, θ\\sin ϕ\\cos ϕ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}\\theta \\cos\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi}$." 13194139534848 false] [17592186119801 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314419 13194139534848 true] [17592186119801 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314426 13194139534848 true] [17592186119801 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314433 13194139534848 true] [17592186119801 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314440 13194139534848 true] [17592186119802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314447 13194139534848 true] [17592186119802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314454 13194139534848 true] [17592186119802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314461 13194139534848 true] [17592186119802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314468 13194139534848 true] [17592186119803 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314475 13194139534848 true] [17592186119803 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314482 13194139534848 true] [17592186119803 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314489 13194139534848 true] [17592186119803 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314496 13194139534848 true] [17592186119804 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314503 13194139534848 true] [17592186119804 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314510 13194139534848 true] [17592186119804 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314517 13194139534848 true] [17592186119988 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534848 true] [17592186119988 1100 "Find a vector-valued function $r(u, v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z = x^{3} - xy$." 13194139534848 false] [17592186119988 1100 "Find a vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z={x}^{3}-xy.$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119988 1145 "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+({x}^{3}-xy)\\,k.\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+({u}^{3}-uv)\\,k\n$$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119989 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534848 true] [17592186119989 1100 "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=2$\nfor $z>0.$ What is the third component of $r?$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119989 1100 "$r(u, v) = ⟨u, v, _____⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u, v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n$x^{3} + y^{2} - z^{2} = 2$\nfor $z > 0$. What is the third component of $r$?" 13194139534848 false] [17592186119989 1145 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z>0.$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2}⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2}.$\n\n**Note:** The parametrization is well-defined for $u$ and $v$ such that ${u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2\\ge 0.$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119990 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534848 true] [17592186119990 1100 "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$z=2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the second component of $f?$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119990 1100 "$f(r, θ) = ⟨r\\cos θ, _____, 2r^{2}\\cos θ⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r, θ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as\n$z = 2r^{2}\\cos θ$.\nWhat is the second component of $f$?" 13194139534848 false] [17592186119990 1145 "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain the parametrization\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119992 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534848 true] [17592186119992 1100 "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$\\rho =ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119992 1100 "$f(θ, ϕ) = ⟨_____, ϕ\\cos θ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, ϕ\\cos θ\\cos ϕ⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(θ, ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as\n$ρ = ϕ\\cos θ$.\nWhat is the first component of $f$?" 13194139534848 false] [17592186119992 1145 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119993 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534848 true] [17592186119993 1100 "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨ϕ\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{ _____ }⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$ϕ=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the third component of $f?$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186119993 1100 "$f(θ, ϕ) = ⟨ϕ\\cot θ\\sin ϕ, ϕ\\sin ϕ, _____⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(θ, ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as\n$ϕ = ρ\\sin θ$.\nWhat is the third component of $f$?" 13194139534848 false] [17592186119993 1145 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $\\rho =f(\\theta ,ϕ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\rho  & =\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $ϕ\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314419 1017 #edb/ref 17592186314420 13194139534848 true] [17592186314419 1089 "q-104138" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314419 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534848 true] [17592186314419 1100 "Find a vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z={e}^{xy}.$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314419 1113 #uuid "f5e0eded-4969-5923-ac06-88f8c9f75ad4" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314419 1145 "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,{e}^{xy}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain a vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,{e}^{uv}⟩\n$$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314421 13194139534848 true] [17592186314420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314422 13194139534848 true] [17592186314420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314423 13194139534848 true] [17592186314420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314424 13194139534848 true] [17592186314420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314425 13194139534848 true] [17592186314420 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534848 true] [17592186314420 1041 "selection" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314420 1104 #edb/ref 17592186314423 13194139534848 true] [17592186314420 1110 #uuid "7ec92c5d-ccbf-5459-a0b6-d47fc8c926b2" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314421 1034 "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,{e}^{u+v}⟩" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314421 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534848 true] [17592186314421 1111 #uuid "5c80d42f-0b98-5c7b-9fda-2a5c79a650cd" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314422 1034 "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡uv⟩" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314422 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534848 true] [17592186314422 1111 #uuid