]{u+v}⟩" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314445 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534848 true] [17592186314445 1111 #uuid "050c8c39-1697-5cdb-b7a6-6ba9ad605971" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314446 1034 "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt[3]{u-v}⟩" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314446 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534848 true] [17592186314446 1111 #uuid "504a1e97-9995-54bd-9a15-d9a53366e7e2" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314447 1017 #edb/ref 17592186314448 13194139534848 true] [17592186314447 1089 "q-104170" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314447 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534848 true] [17592186314447 1100 "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-{z}^{2}=1$\nfor $z>0.$ What is the third component of $r?$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314447 1113 #uuid "6d37951d-f75b-50a7-a043-80a7f31b132e" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314447 1145 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z>0.$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1}⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}.$\n\n**Note:** The parametrization is well-defined for $u$ and $v$ such that ${u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1\\ge 0.$" 13194139534848 true] [17592186314448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314449 13194139534848 true] [17592186314448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314450 13194139534848 true] [17592186314448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314451 13194139534848 true] [17592186314448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314452 1319